       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. You confirmed the dose, but you didn't give a clinical reason, and I can't think of a therapeutic one. 50 mg is five times the maximum labeled dose, and in a 70 kg patient with normal kidney and liver function it would be an overdose. It could cause profound hypotension and shock that lasts a long time.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** Give me 5 mg or 10 mg once daily, or another dose you choose, and I'll enter it right away.\n- **Treat refractory hypertension another way.** If 10 mg isn't enough, the usual next step is to add another agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide, rather than raise the amlodipine dose.\n- **Check for a typo or unit error.** For example, you may have meant 5 mg.\n- **Get a second review.** If you believe 50 mg is intended, please discuss it with the pharmacist and attending first. It would then need to go through whatever override or verification process your hospital requires.\n\nWhich dose should I enter?"
      }
     ],
